    "result_text": "All 282 tests pass, including the new `Cp17Tests`. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` flag on owner creation. When an owner is created, the response's `sharesHousehold` boolean is `true` if another owner already had the same address and city at registration, otherwise `false`. Creation still proceeds unconditionally (no rejection step added).\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `sharesHousehold` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field and getter/setter.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `@Mapping(target = \"sharesHousehold\", ignore = true)` on `toOwnerDto`, plus a static `sharesHousehold(owner, allOwners)` helper. It follows the same id-based pattern as the existing computed fields (`namesakeCount`, `customerCode`, etc.): true when any owner with a smaller id shares this owner's address and city (both matched case-insensitively). Because a new owner always has the largest id, this reflects owners that existed *before* this one and stays stable when read back via GET.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwnerCreated`, `RespondWithOwner` (GET), `RespondWithOwnerUpdated`** \u2014 each now sets `dto.setSharesHousehold(...)` alongside the other computed fields, so the flag appears consistently on the create, fetch, and update responses.\n\nThe test creates owner A at a fresh address/city (flag `false`), then creates owner B at the same address/city and reads it back via GET (flag `true`) \u2014 both assertions pass.",
